course: dictionary
lesson: 5853
title: Finding meaning in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A mallard is a wild duck. You can spot one on ponds and rivers in Britain, Ireland, North America, Australia and New Zealand. Most farm ducks come from mallards.
explain: Read the clues in «In spring a mallard mother marches a wobbly line of ducklings across the path». The words mother and ducklings tell you that a mallard is a grown-up duck with baby ducks following her.
explain: Test your idea by swapping in a simpler word. «A duck mother marches a wobbly line of ducklings» still makes sense, so mallard means a kind of duck.
fact: A mallard is the most common wild duck.
fact: The male mallard is called a drake. He has a shiny green head, a white ring round his neck and a grey body.
fact: The female mallard is speckled brown, which helps her hide on the nest.
fact: «Mallard» is also the name of a famous British steam train that set the world speed record for steam in 1938, at 126 mph.
fact: «A pair of mallards» means two mallards, and «mallard ducklings» means baby mallards.
exercise: What is a mallard? | *A kind of wild duck | A large bird that lives on the sea | A type of tree | A kind of fish
exercise: «The male mallard has a shiny green head.» What is a male mallard called? | A swan | *A drake | A duckling | A mother
exercise: «A pair of mallards» means how many mallards? | *Two | Ten | Five | One
test: «The female mallard is speckled brown.» Which feature helps her? | She has a white ring round her neck | She can fly faster than a train | She has a shiny green head | *She can hide on the nest
test: In 1938 the steam train «Mallard» set what record? | The most ducklings in one nest | The longest train line | *A world speed record for steam | The biggest pond in Britain
exam: In «Ducks like mallards... tipping their heads under the water and their tails up to nibble plants», what are the mallards doing? | *To feed on plants on the bottom | To hiss at swans | To fly high in the sky | To hatch their eggs
